<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Watt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1736">1736</a>–<a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Watt was a Scottish inventor and instrument maker whose improvements made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a> practical for industry. His separate condenser, patented in 1769, greatly reduced fuel consumption, and in partnership with Matthew Boulton at Birmingham from 1775 he built engines that drove mills and pumped mines. The unit of power, the watt, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-watt.jpg" alt="James Watt" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Frederik von Breda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Watt_James_von_Breda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Watt was born in Greenock, Renfrewshire, in January 1736, the eldest of the five surviving children of Agnes Muirhead and James Watt, a shipwright, ship owner and contractor who served as Greenock's chief baillie in 1751. The family's wealth came in part from his father's trading in slaves and slave-produced goods. His parents were Presbyterians and strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a>, but he later became a deist. He was educated at home by his mother and then at Greenock Grammar School, where he showed an aptitude for mathematics while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek failed to interest him. After leaving school he worked in his father's workshops, and when his father's ventures failed he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> to find employment as a mathematical instrument maker.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1759 his friend John Robison called his attention to steam as a source of motive power. The Newcomen engine had been used for almost fifty years to pump water from mines and had hardly changed. Watt began to experiment, though he had never seen an operating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a>, and came to realise the importance of latent heat, which his friend Joseph Black had discovered years before. His insight was that contemporary engines wasted energy by repeatedly cooling and reheating the cylinder, and he introduced the separate condenser to avoid this. Eventually he adapted the engine to produce rotary motion, which greatly broadened its use beyond pumping water.</p><p>Watt had great financial difficulties until he entered a partnership with Matthew Boulton in 1775. The first engines were installed in commercial enterprises in 1776, and for the next five years he installed more, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. Boulton and Watt charged an annual payment equal to one-third of the value of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> saved compared with a Newcomen engine. Mine owners in Cornwall became convinced that his patent could not be enforced and withheld payments, and Watt had to go to court. The Soho Foundry formally opened in 1796, and in 1800 Watt retired, the year his fundamental patent and the partnership expired.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The story that Watt was inspired by a boiling kettle is probably apocryphal, possibly created by his son James Watt Jr.; Watt did not invent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a> but improved the existing Newcomen engine. He also devised a letter copying machine and studied chemistry, finding that a solution of chlorine could bleach textiles. In retirement he built machines for copying sculptures, which he never patented. He was a member of the Lunar Society of Birmingham. The chemist Humphry Davy said that those who considered him only a practical mechanic formed an erroneous idea of him, since he was equally distinguished as a natural philosopher and a chemist. He died on 25 August 1819, and the SI unit of power, the watt, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Watt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1760s the Industrial Revolution began, with James Hargreaves’s spinning jenny, Richard Arkwright’s water frame and factories, <b>James Watt</b>’s improved steam engine, patented in 1769, and canals such as the Bridgewater Canal of 1761.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Greenock, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Heathfield Hall, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-4.77&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Greenock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James Watt"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4772 5338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4772 5338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-4.77&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
